<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kim Jong Il</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/2011">2011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kim Jong Il was a North Korean politician and dictator who was the second supreme leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> from the death of his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a>, in 1994 until his own death in 2011. Born in the far east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, he rose through the Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, helped develop the Juche ideology and the cult of personality, and was designated his father's successor in 1980. He took power amid a catastrophic economic crisis and famine, ruled a repressive and totalitarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> and made the army central through his Songun (military-first) policies. North Korea conducted its first nuclear test in 2006 under his rule; he promoted his third son, Kim Jong Un, as his successor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kim-jong-il.jpg" alt="Kim Jong Il" width="330" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jesse Charlie · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kim_Jong_il_Portrait-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 1980s <b>Kim Jong Il</b> sought to reduce the song's importance in favour of the &quot;Song of General Kim Il Sung&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aegukka">Aegukka (North Korea)</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;No Motherland Without You&quot;, also called &quot;Ode to <b>Kim Jong Il</b>&quot;, is a North Korean song about the country's second supreme leader, Kim Jong Il.</blockquote><a href="/songs/no-motherland-without-you">No Motherland Without You</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1980s <b>Kim Jong Il</b> began promoting the song, and it has since displaced &quot;Aegukka&quot;, the national anthem, as the most important song in the country and the de facto anthem played at public gatherings, and it has been sung more than the official anthem since…</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-general-kim-il-sung">Song of General Kim Il Sung</a></li><li><blockquote>It praises <b>Kim Jong Il</b>, who ruled from 1994 to 2011 and is styled the &quot;Eternal General Secretary&quot;, as part of his personality cult; under his father Kim Il Sung he had himself helped to run the cult of the elder Kim.</blockquote><a href="/songs/song-of-general-kim-jong-il">Song of General Kim Jong Il</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words are attributed to <b>Kim Jong Il</b>, later the country's Supreme Leader; the Korean article describes him as its presumed composer.</blockquote><a href="/songs/where-are-you-dear-general">Where Are You, Dear General?</a></li></ul></section>
